1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 8.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:06,250
1, Disks, Volumes and RAID, the start of
Learning Plan 8.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,100
Every file server starts with the same
job: turn blank drives into safe, usable

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:13,550
storage.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:19,800
You will compare hard drives, SSDs and
NVMe drives, and the three ways to connect

6
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:22,050
storage: DAS, NAS and SAN.

7
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,500
You will choose MBR or GPT and see why
dynamic disks are out.

8
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:33,750
You will pick NTFS or ReFS, create, extend
and shrink volumes in Disk Management and

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,400
PowerShell, and calculate what RAID gives
you and what it costs.

10
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:41,050
Servers use three kinds of drives.

11
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:47,300
A hard disk drive, or HDD, has spinning
platters, at 7,200, 10,000 or 15,000 rpm.

12
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:52,750
It is cheap per terabyte but slow, so it
suits archives and backups.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,400
An SSD stores data in flash memory with no
moving parts, so it is much faster.

14
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:04,050
Flash wears out with writes, so SSDs carry
an endurance rating.

15
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:10,300
NVMe SSDs talk over PCI Express lanes
instead of SATA or SAS, for the lowest

16
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:11,750
delay.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,800
Hot-swap bays let you replace a failed
drive while the server runs.

18
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:23,050
Direct-attached storage, or DAS, is inside
the server or on a shelf connected with a

19
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:24,500
SAS cable.

20
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:26,750
Only that server uses it.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,200
The four data disks on SRV01 are DAS.

22
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:36,050
Network-attached storage, or NAS, is a box
that shares folders over the network with

23
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,500
SMB or NFS.

24
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:40,950
That is file storage, used in Lab 8.

25
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,400
3.

26
00:01:42,650 --> 00:01:47,850
A storage area network, or SAN, gives
servers whole disks, called LUNs, over

27
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:49,700
iSCSI or Fibre Channel.

28
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:55,550
That is block storage that looks like a
local disk, used in Lab 8.

29
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,000
4.

30
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,850
Here is the disk you build in the lab.

31
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:05,100
A disk first gets a partition style, MBR
or GPT.

32
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:07,750
Then you cut it into partitions.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,400
A partition with a file system on it, such
as NTFS or ReFS, is a volume.

34
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:19,850
Then you give it a drive letter like E, or
a mount point.

35
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:27,300
A mount point makes a volume appear as an
empty folder on an NTFS volume, such as E

36
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:28,750
Archive.

37
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,400
Windows may also add a small reserved
partition with no letter.

38
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:37,650
MBR, the Master Boot Record, is the old
partition style.

39
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:44,300
It can only use 2 terabytes of a disk and
holds four primary partitions, or three

40
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:47,350
plus an extended partition with logical
drives.

41
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,800
GPT, the GUID Partition Table, handles
disks far larger than anything sold today

42
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:55,850
and up to 128 partitions in Windows.

43
00:02:56,100 --> 00:03:01,700
It keeps a backup copy of its table, and
UEFI computers boot from it.

44
00:03:01,950 --> 00:03:07,150
GPT is the default in Disk Management and
in Initialize-Disk, so choose GPT.

45
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,200
Changing later is hard, because Disk
Management converts only an empty disk.

46
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:15,250
Every new disk is a basic disk.

47
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:21,500
It holds partitions and simple volumes,
and that is all you need in this course.

48
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:26,550
Dynamic disks are the old way Windows
built spanned, striped, mirrored and

49
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:28,800
RAID-5 volumes across several disks.

50
00:03:29,050 --> 00:03:33,850
Microsoft now calls dynamic disks
deprecated and does not recommend them for

51
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:35,300
new deployments.

52
00:03:35,550 --> 00:03:41,550
You may still find them on old servers,
and going back to basic means deleting

53
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,000
every volume.

54
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:47,250
Instead, pool disks with Storage Spaces,
which is Topic 8.

55
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,300
2, or use a hardware RAID controller.

56
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,750
Windows Server gives you two main file
systems.

57
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
NTFS is the general-purpose one.

58
00:03:56,250 --> 00:03:59,450
It can boot, so C is always NTFS.

59
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:04,100
It can shrink, and it supports disk quotas
and removable media.

60
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:08,350
ReFS, the Resilient File System, is built
for big data.

61
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:13,400
It handles volumes up to 35 petabytes,
against 256 terabytes for NTFS.

62
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:18,050
Block clone copies large files almost
instantly, and integrity streams keep

63
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:19,900
checksums of file data.

64
00:04:20,150 --> 00:04:22,950
But ReFS cannot boot and cannot shrink.

65
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:26,000
Both support permissions, BitLocker,
deduplication and extending.

66
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:30,650
Use NTFS by default, and ReFS for VMs,
backups and archives.

67
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:33,300
Disk Management is the graphical tool.

68
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:35,950
Right-click Start and choose Disk
Management.

69
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:42,600
In the lower pane, find the new disk by
its size and state: 20 gigabytes, Unknown,

70
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:44,050
Offline.

71
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,900
Numbers can change, so do not trust them
alone.

72
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:53,350
Right-click the grey label on the left,
not the bar, and choose Online.

73
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,000
The disk now says Not Initialized.

74
00:04:56,250 --> 00:04:59,450
Right-click the label again and choose
Initialize Disk.

75
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:05,300
In the dialog, check that only your disk
is selected, choose GPT, GUID Partition

76
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:07,150
Table, and select OK.

77
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:10,200
The disk becomes Basic, with unallocated
space.

78
00:05:10,450 --> 00:05:14,450
Now right-click the unallocated space and
choose New Simple Volume.

79
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:18,300
On Specify Volume Size, the size is in
megabytes.

80
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:22,150
Ten gigabytes is 10 times 1024, so type
10240.

81
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:28,800
The next page assigns a drive letter, E in
our lab, or a mount point folder.

82
00:05:29,050 --> 00:05:33,850
On Format Partition, choose the file
system, NTFS here, keep the default

83
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:37,700
allocation unit size, and type a volume
label, Data.

84
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:39,550
Leave quick format on.

85
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:45,800
Select Next and Finish, and in a few
seconds Data E appears, healthy and ready.

86
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:50,050
The same job in PowerShell takes a handful
of cmdlets.

87
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:54,300
Get-Disk, filtered on partition style RAW,
lists the blank disks.

88
00:05:54,550 --> 00:05:59,750
Set-Disk with IsOffline false brings the
disk online, and IsReadOnly false makes it

89
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,200
writable.

90
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:05,050
Initialize-Disk with partition style GPT
writes the partition table.

91
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:11,300
Then New-Partition with a size of 10GB and
drive letter E creates the partition, and

92
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:16,750
you pipe it straight into Format-Volume
with the file system and the label.

93
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,600
In the lab a variable holds the disk, so
you never mistype its number.

94
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:24,850
Volumes can grow and shrink.

95
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:27,900
Right-click a volume and choose Extend
Volume.

96
00:06:28,150 --> 00:06:31,350
The wizard asks how many megabytes to add.

97
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:37,600
The unallocated space must be on the same
disk and right after the volume, with

98
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:39,050
nothing in between.

99
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:42,500
NTFS and ReFS volumes can both be
extended.

100
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:45,150
Shrink Volume works the other way.

101
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:51,800
Windows first checks how much space it can
free, then you type the amount to shrink

102
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:53,250
in megabytes.

103
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:59,900
Only NTFS volumes can shrink, and files
that cannot move, such as a page file, set

104
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:01,350
the limit.

105
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:03,600
In PowerShell, start with
Get-PartitionSupportedSize.

106
00:07:03,850 --> 00:07:09,850
It shows SizeMin and SizeMax in bytes, the
smallest and largest the partition can be

107
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:11,300
right now.

108
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:13,550
Stay between those two values.

109
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:18,200
Resize-Partition with a bigger size
extends the volume, here to 12GB.

110
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:22,450
Resize-Partition with a smaller size
shrinks it, back to 10GB.

111
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:28,700
To check, read the partition's Size
property and divide by 1GB, so you see a

112
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:30,550
simple 12 or 10.

113
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:35,600
PowerShell understands suffixes like GB
and MB, while Disk Management always asks

114
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:37,050
in megabytes.

115
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:41,700
RAID makes several drives act as one, a
big Server+ topic.

116
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:44,350
RAID 0 stripes data across drives.

117
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:49,000
It is fast, but if any drive fails,
everything is lost.

118
00:07:49,250 --> 00:07:54,450
RAID 1 mirrors: two drives hold the same
data, so either can fail.

119
00:07:54,700 --> 00:08:00,300
RAID 5 stripes data with parity, a
calculation that can rebuild a lost block,

120
00:08:00,550 --> 00:08:02,950
spread over at least three drives.

121
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:08,800
RAID 6 keeps two parity blocks, so it
needs four drives and survives two

122
00:08:09,050 --> 00:08:10,250
failures.

123
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:14,500
RAID 10 stripes across mirrored pairs, for
speed and safety.

124
00:08:14,750 --> 00:08:21,950
Here are the formulas, where n is the
number of drives and s is the size of one.

125
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:26,200
RAID 0 gives you all of it, n times s.

126
00:08:26,450 --> 00:08:28,850
RAID 1 gives one drive's worth.

127
00:08:29,100 --> 00:08:34,700
RAID 5 gives n minus 1 times s, and RAID 6
n minus 2.

128
00:08:34,950 --> 00:08:36,550
RAID 10 gives half.

129
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:41,200
Six 4 terabyte drives give 24, 20, 16 or
12 terabytes.

130
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:47,450
One small write on RAID 5 means reading
old data and parity, then writing both:

131
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:48,900
four operations.

132
00:08:49,150 --> 00:08:50,750
RAID 6 needs six.

133
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,400
RAID 1 and 10 need two.

134
00:08:53,650 --> 00:08:57,650
A hot spare is an extra drive that sits
idle.

135
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:02,700
When a drive fails, the array rebuilds
onto the spare at once.

136
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:04,550
It adds no capacity.

137
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:10,800
A hardware RAID controller is a card with
its own processor and cache, and Windows

138
00:09:11,050 --> 00:09:12,650
sees just one disk.

139
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:17,700
In write-back mode, it reports done once
data is in its cache.

140
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:22,750
A battery or a flash module protects that
cache if power fails.

141
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:26,600
Software RAID, like Storage Spaces, uses
the server's CPU.

142
00:09:26,850 --> 00:09:29,650
And remember: RAID is not a backup.

143
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:31,100
Four common mistakes.

144
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:34,550
One: choosing a disk by its number alone.

145
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:39,200
Numbers change, so match the size and the
RAW state first.

146
00:09:39,450 --> 00:09:41,850
Two: treating RAID as a backup.

147
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:48,900
RAID keeps you running when a drive dies,
but a deleted or encrypted file is gone on

148
00:09:49,150 --> 00:09:50,350
every drive.

149
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:56,200
Three: using ReFS for C, or for a volume
you may need to shrink.

150
00:09:56,450 --> 00:10:00,050
ReFS cannot boot or shrink and has no
quotas.

151
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:06,300
Four: trying to extend into free space
that is not right next to the volume.

152
00:10:06,550 --> 00:10:08,150
Plan your layout first.

153
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:10,000
Time to check yourself.

154
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:15,050
First, a new 6 terabyte disk must hold one
5 terabyte volume.

155
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:18,500
Do you choose MBR or GPT, and why?

156
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:22,350
Second, five 8 terabyte drives are in RAID
5.

157
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:27,000
What is the usable capacity, and how many
drives can fail?

158
00:10:27,250 --> 00:10:31,650
Third, you want to shrink F, which is
formatted with ReFS.

159
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:33,100
What happens?

160
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:38,150
Fourth, a file server must keep running if
any two drives fail.

161
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:44,400
Which RAID level do you choose, and how
many drives do you need at least?

162
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:46,650
Pause the video and answer.

163
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:48,500
Here are the answers.

164
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:49,950
One: GPT.

165
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:57,400
MBR can only use 2 terabytes of a disk,
while GPT uses all 6, and it is the

166
00:10:57,650 --> 00:10:58,850
default anyway.

167
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:02,300
Two: 32 terabytes, and one drive can fail.

168
00:11:02,550 --> 00:11:07,350
RAID 5 gives n minus 1 times s, so 4 times
8.

169
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:10,400
Three: you cannot shrink a ReFS volume.

170
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:16,650
Copy the data off, delete the volume and
create it again at the new size.

171
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:20,100
Four: RAID 6, with at least four drives.

172
00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:22,750
Double parity survives any two failures.

173
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,400
Let me show you on SRV01.

174
00:11:25,650 --> 00:11:30,850
In Disk Management, the four new disks
show 20 gigabytes, Unknown and Offline,

175
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:33,500
and Get-Disk shows them as RAW.

176
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:40,150
I bring the first one online, initialize
it as GPT, and create E, Data, NTFS, 10240

177
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:41,600
megabytes.

178
00:11:41,850 --> 00:11:49,050
Now I extend E by 2 gigabytes, check it
shows 12, and shrink it back to 10 with

179
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:50,500
Resize-Partition.

180
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:53,550
Next, PowerShell creates F, Archive, with
ReFS.

181
00:11:53,800 --> 00:12:00,200
Watch the menus: F cannot shrink, and E
can no longer extend, because F sits right

182
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:01,650
after it.

183
00:12:01,900 --> 00:12:03,900
E has a Quota tab.

184
00:12:04,150 --> 00:12:05,350
F does not.

185
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:06,800
To wrap up.

186
00:12:07,050 --> 00:12:13,450
Hard drives give capacity, SSDs and NVMe
give speed, and storage can be DAS, NAS or

187
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:14,900
SAN.

188
00:12:15,150 --> 00:12:19,150
Use GPT and basic disks, because dynamic
disks are deprecated.

189
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:25,800
Use NTFS for general volumes and C, and
ReFS for big data, knowing it cannot boot

190
00:12:26,050 --> 00:12:27,250
or shrink.

191
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:30,700
Bring disks online, initialize, partition
and format them.

192
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:34,150
Extend needs free space right after the
volume.

193
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:38,800
RAID trades capacity for safety, and it is
never a backup.

194
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:40,250
Lab 8.

195
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:44,900
1 takes about 75 minutes on SRV01, plus a
RAID worksheet.
